Ladybird now lays the decorations of vertical text out along the run of
its glyphs and turns them with the glyphs, and lines up the decorations
of upright glyphs with the edges of their em box. Run in Chrome 158,
Firefox 151 and WebKit 26.5 with Latin, Japanese and mixed text in every
vertical writing mode, all three draw underlines down the line-under
side, overlines down the line-over side and line-throughs down the
middle, and honor text-underline-position: left and right; Ladybird now
matches Chrome on all twelve cases of the probe, including one straight
underline under mixed Latin and Japanese text. Blink paints decorations
in line-relative coordinates and maps them to physical ones with the
same LineRelativeRect transform as the text, which is the approach taken
here; Gecko’s nsTextFrame passes the run’s verticality and sidewaysLeft
to nsCSSRendering::PaintDecorationLine(). Six more WPT css-text-decor
vertical tests pass. Ink isn’t skipped around upright glyphs yet, so
text-decoration-skip-ink-upright-001 fails, and test-web’s font has
descenders too short for the sideways skip-ink tests to tell anything.
